Event delegation is the technique of attaching a single event listener to a PARENT element instead of multiple listeners to each child element.

How it works: Events bubble up from child to parent in the DOM tree. The parent listener can inspect event.target to determine which child was clicked.

Why it matters:
1. Performance: 1 listener vs 1000 listeners (for a list of 1000 items)
2. Dynamic content: New children added later automatically work (no re-binding)
3. Memory: Less event listeners = less memory usage

Example:
// BAD: 100 listeners for 100 buttons
buttons.forEach(btn => btn.addEventListener('click', handler));

// GOOD: 1 listener on container
container.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
  const btn = e.target.closest('[data-action]');
  if (!btn) return;
  handleAction(btn.dataset.action);
});

Web Workers run JavaScript in a background thread — separate from the main thread. Since JavaScript is single-threaded, heavy computation on the main thread blocks UI rendering and user interactions.

Web Workers:
- Have no access to the DOM
- Communicate via postMessage() / onmessage
- Run in a completely isolated context

When to use:
1. Image/video processing (pixel manipulation, compression)
2. Cryptography operations (key generation, hashing)
3. Parsing large datasets (CSV, JSON)
4. Complex physics/game simulations
5. Real-time code syntax highlighting

Example:
// main.js
const worker = new Worker('/workers/crypto.js');
worker.postMessage({ action: 'hash', data: largeFile });
worker.onmessage = (e) => console.log('Hash:', e.data.result);

// workers/crypto.js
self.onmessage = async (e) => {
  const hash = await crypto.subtle.digest('SHA-256', e.data.data);
  self.postMessage({ result: hex(hash) });
};
